# The Tiger's Day — Java Game Server

## Structure

```
tigers-day/
├── build.gradle
├── README.md
└── src/main/java/com/tigersday/
    ├── Main.java          ← entry point
    ├── GameServer.java    ← WebSocket server (port 8887)
    ├── GameState.java     ← turn/phase/victory logic
    ├── GameBoard.java     ← board topology (nodes + edges)
    ├── Territory.java     ← data object for each node
    └── MoveResult.java    ← result returned after every action
└── src/main/resources/
    └── tiger-day.html     ← browser client with WebSocket bridge
```

## Prerequisites

- Java 17+
- Gradle 8+ (or use the wrapper: `./gradlew`)

## Run

```bash
# From the tigers-day/ directory:
./gradlew run
```

Server starts at `ws://localhost:8887`.

Open `src/main/resources/tiger-day.html` in a browser.
The status bar at the top of the sidebar will turn green when connected.

## How to play

1. Click a territory you control (selection ring appears).
2. Click an adjacent enemy or empty territory to move there.
3. The server validates the move, updates ownership, and broadcasts the new state.
4. Victory check runs after every move.

## WebSocket message protocol

### Browser → Java
| type   | fields                                      |
|--------|---------------------------------------------|
| MOVE   | player ("british"\|"mysore"), from, to      |
| PASS   | player                                      |
| RESET  | —                                           |
| PING   | —                                           |

### Java → Browser
| type        | fields                                              |
|-------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
| STATE       | turn, maxTurns, phase, winner, board[]              |
| MOVE_RESULT | status, reason, turn, phase, winner?, board[]       |
| PONG        | —                                                   |
| ERROR       | message                                             |

## Adding an AI player

Add an `AiPlayer` class that calls `state.applyMove("mysore", from, to)` directly
(no WebSocket needed — it's in the same JVM). After the move, call
`server.broadcastState()` to push the new state to all connected browsers.